[38;2;124;124;124msubgroup, specifically whether it contains a normal subgroup isomorphic to Z2.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mG2 is the smallest of the five exceptional compact Lie groups. It's a 14-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional compact, connected, simply-connected simple Lie group.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. G2 is a simple Lie group - its Lie algebra g2 is simple (one of the five [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexceptional simple Lie algebras).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The compact form of G2 is connected and simply-connected.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the question is about normal subgroups of the compact Lie group G2 (not [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mjust the Lie algebra).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a connected compact Lie group, there's a correspondence between closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnormal subgroups and ideals of the Lie algebra. But the question asks about [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnormal subgroups in general (not necessarily closed).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think more carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, closed normal subgroups: If G is a connected compact Lie group with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msimple Lie algebra g, then any closed normal subgroup N of G is either finite [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(central) or all of G. Since the Lie algebra is simple, the only closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconnected normal subgroups are {e} and G itself. A closed normal subgroup that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis not connected would have a connected component that is also normal, so it [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmust be finite and central (contained in the center).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe center of the compact, simply-connected G2: The center of a simply-connected[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcompact simple Lie group is the kernel of the map to the adjoint group. For G2, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe center is trivial. This is because G2 is both simply-connected AND has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtrivial center (it's its own adjoint group). The Dynkin diagram of G2 has no [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnontrivial symmetries, and the fundamental group of the adjoint group G2_ad is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtrivial, meaning G2 is already simply-connected and equals its adjoint form.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the center of G2 is trivial.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, any closed normal subgroup of G2 is trivial.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut what about non-closed normal subgroups? The question asks about "nontrivial [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnormal subgroup" without specifying closed.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, this is the subtle part. A normal subgroup isomorphic to Z2 would be a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubgroup of order 2. Any subgroup of order 2 is finite, hence closed (in a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHausdorff topological group, finite sets are closed). So if there were a normal [0m[38;2;124;124;124msubgroup isomorphic to Z2, it would be closed and finite, hence central (since a[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfinite normal subgroup of a connected group is central).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me verify: a finite normal subgroup of a connected topological group [0m
